{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (days 1–10): model is slightly low on up-moves and slightly high on down-moves; apply a small trend-following adjustment toward the most recent realized direction (~+0.2 to +0.6 €/t when the last 3–5 days are rising; ~-0.2 to -0.6 €/t when falling).",
    "Mid-horizon (days ~12–22): model shows a consistent positive bias (over-forecasting levels by roughly +0.5 to +2.0 €/t); subtract ~1 €/t baseline in this window, larger (~1.5 €/t) if the model path is elevated/plateauing near local highs.",
    "Turning points / mean reversion: model lags reversals—keeps prices too high after peaks and too low after troughs; dampen forecast slope after sharp 1–3 day moves by pulling day+3 to day+15 forecasts 20–40% back toward the last observed price (mean-reversion blend).",
    "Late-horizon (days ~23–30): model often overshoots downside (predicts too deep/too early drops) and misses rebound; cap drawdowns and add rebound bias by shifting late-horizon forecasts upward by ~+0.5 to +3 €/t when the model projects a sustained decline below recent range.",
    "Volatility exaggeration: model amplitude is too large (highs too high, lows too low) vs realized; apply a variance shrink (e.g., scale deviations from last price by ~0.7–0.85) especially beyond day 10."
  ]
}
